---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';

/**
 * /viz/hasse-diagrams/ — one gallery entry for the family of poset maps
 * built for Math 372 (fall 2026). The maps themselves stay standalone
 * pages under public/<slug>/; this page explains what they have in common
 * and links out to each, so the gallery carries one card instead of four.
 *
 * The maps are listed once, below. To add or retitle one, edit that array.
 *
 * public/thumbs/hasse-diagrams.png is a 1200x900 render of the inline figure
 * below on the light palette, not a product of `npm run thumbs` (which only
 * shoots public/<slug>/ pages). Re-render it by hand if the figure changes.
 */
const maps = [
  {
    slug: 'graph-poset-map',
    title: 'Unlabeled graphs',
    formula: 'B<sub>C(<i>n</i>,2)</sub>/<i>S<sub>n</sub></i>',
    href: '/graph-poset-map/',
    thumb: '/thumbs/graph-poset-map.png',
    blurb:
      'Graphs on <i>n</i> ≤ 7 vertices up to isomorphism, ordered by “is isomorphic to a subgraph of.” ' +
      'The symmetric group acts on the Boolean algebra of edge sets, and the orbits are the graphs. ' +
      'Layouts by complement symmetry, degree spread, or |Aut|; a panel with automorphisms, degree sequence, covers, and the complement.',
    tags: ['graphs', 'posets'],
  },
  {
    slug: 'necklace-poset-map',
    title: 'Necklaces',
    formula: '[<i>k</i>−1]<sup><i>n</i></sup>/<i>C<sub>n</sub></i> and [<i>k</i>−1]<sup><i>n</i></sup>/<i>D<sub>n</sub></i>',
    href: '/necklace-poset-map/',
    thumb: '/thumbs/necklace-poset-map.png',
    blurb:
      'Necklaces or bracelets of <i>n</i> beads in 2, 3, or 4 colors, ordered bead by bead: a product of chains modulo the cyclic or dihedral group. ' +
      'With two colors this is exactly Stanley’s B<sub><i>n</i></sub>/<i>C<sub>n</sub></i>. ' +
      'The panel gives the stabilizer, orbit size, color runs, covers, and the complementary necklace.',
    tags: ['necklaces', 'posets'],
  },
  {
    slug: 'simplicial-complex-map',
    title: 'Simplicial complexes',
    formula: '<i>J</i>(B<sub><i>n</i></sub>)/<i>S<sub>n</sub></i>',
    href: '/simplicial-complex-map/',
    thumb: '/thumbs/simplicial-complex-map.png',
    blurb:
      'Simplicial complexes on <i>n</i> ≤ 5 vertices up to isomorphism, ordered by “is isomorphic to a subcomplex of”: the lattice of down-sets of B<sub><i>n</i></sub>, modulo <i>S<sub>n</sub></i>. ' +
      'Rank is the number of faces, and the order-reversing symmetry is Alexander duality. ' +
      'The width of each quotient is computed, so you can check Sperner’s property by eye.',
    tags: ['simplicial complexes', 'posets'],
  },
  {
    slug: 'finite-space-map',
    title: 'Finite spaces',
    formula: 'topologies on [<i>n</i>], modulo <i>S<sub>n</sub></i>',
    href: '/finite-space-map/',
    thumb: '/thumbs/finite-space-map.png',
    blurb:
      'Topological spaces on <i>n</i> ≤ 6 points up to homeomorphism, ordered from coarser to finer: the poset of topologies on [<i>n</i>] modulo <i>S<sub>n</sub></i>. ' +
      'Each space is drawn as its open sets marked on B<sub><i>n</i></sub> or as its specialization preorder; the mirror symmetry swaps open and closed sets. ' +
      'Rows count open sets, but the order is not graded — covers jump rows — so this is the one map where Sperner’s machinery does not apply.',
    tags: ['finite spaces', 'posets'],
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="Hasse diagrams · Kyle Ormsby"
  description="Zoomable Hasse diagrams of the quotient posets from Stanley's Chapter 5 — unlabeled graphs, necklaces, simplicial complexes, and finite spaces — and a primer on reading them."
  wide
>
  <div style="max-width:var(--measure)">
    <p class="label">visualizations · posets</p>
    <h1 style="margin-top:var(--s3)">Hasse diagrams</h1>
    <p class="lede dim" style="margin-top:var(--s4)">
      Four maps of quotient posets — unlabeled graphs, necklaces, simplicial
      complexes, finite spaces — drawn large enough to wander around in.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose-col" style="margin-top:var(--s7)">
    <p>
      A finite poset is drawn by its <em>cover relation</em>: one dot per
      element, and a segment from <i>x</i> up to <i>y</i> exactly when{' '}
      <i>x</i> &lt; <i>y</i> with nothing in between. Transitivity supplies
      the rest, so the picture is as sparse as the order allows, and height
      reads as rank. That drawing is the Hasse diagram, and for anything much
      bigger than the Boolean algebra B<sub>4</sub> it stops fitting on a
      page — which is where the maps below come in.
    </p>
    <p>
      They all draw the same kind of object, the quotient poset{' '}
      <i>P</i>/<i>G</i> of Stanley’s{' '}
      <a href="https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/algebraic-combinatorics/"><em>Algebraic Combinatorics</em></a>,
      Chapter 5. Start with a poset <i>P</i> — subsets of [<i>n</i>], a
      product of chains, the down-sets of B<sub><i>n</i></sub>, the
      topologies on [<i>n</i>] — and a group <i>G</i> acting on it by
      automorphisms. The elements of <i>P</i>/<i>G</i> are the orbits, and
      one orbit lies below another when some representative of the first lies
      below some representative of the second. Unlabeled graphs are the orbits
      of <i>S<sub>n</sub></i> on edge sets; necklaces are the orbits of{' '}
      <i>C<sub>n</sub></i> on colorings; finite spaces are the orbits of{' '}
      <i>S<sub>n</sub></i> on topologies.
    </p>
  </div>

  <figure class="hasse-fig">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 760 232" role="img"
         aria-label="Three Hasse diagrams: the Boolean algebra B₃ drawn as a cube, its quotient by S₃ drawn as a chain with orbit sizes 1, 3, 3, 1 and cover multiplicities 1, 2, 3, and the quotient B₄/C₄ drawn as a diamond of six elements.">
      <g class="edges" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-linecap="round">
        <!-- B_3 -->
        <line x1="110" y1="196" x2="50" y2="140"/><line x1="110" y1="196" x2="110" y2="140"/><line x1="110" y1="196" x2="170" y2="140"/>
        <line x1="50" y1="140" x2="50" y2="84"/><line x1="50" y1="140" x2="110" y2="84"/>
        <line x1="110" y1="140" x2="50" y2="84"/><line x1="110" y1="140" x2="170" y2="84"/>
        <line x1="170" y1="140" x2="110" y2="84"/><line x1="170" y1="140" x2="170" y2="84"/>
        <line x1="50" y1="84" x2="110" y2="28"/><line x1="110" y1="84" x2="110" y2="28"/><line x1="170" y1="84" x2="110" y2="28"/>
        <!-- B_3 / S_3 -->
        <line x1="330" y1="196" x2="330" y2="140"/><line x1="330" y1="140" x2="330" y2="84"/><line x1="330" y1="84" x2="330" y2="28"/>
        <!-- B_4 / C_4 -->
        <line x1="600" y1="212" x2="600" y2="166"/>
        <line x1="600" y1="166" x2="560" y2="120"/><line x1="600" y1="166" x2="640" y2="120"/>
        <line x1="560" y1="120" x2="600" y2="74"/><line x1="640" y1="120" x2="600" y2="74"/>
        <line x1="600" y1="74" x2="600" y2="28"/>
      </g>
      <!-- quotient arrow -->
      <g class="arrow" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.2">
        <line x1="212" y1="112" x2="266" y2="112"/><polyline points="259,106 266,112 259,118"/>
      </g>
      <g class="nodes" fill="var(--paper)" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4">
        <circle cx="110" cy="196" r="4.5"/><circle cx="50" cy="140" r="4.5"/><circle cx="110" cy="140" r="4.5"/><circle cx="170" cy="140" r="4.5"/>
        <circle cx="50" cy="84" r="4.5"/><circle cx="110" cy="84" r="4.5"/><circle cx="170" cy="84" r="4.5"/><circle cx="110" cy="28" r="4.5"/>
        <circle cx="330" cy="196" r="4.5"/><circle cx="330" cy="140" r="4.5"/><circle cx="330" cy="84" r="4.5"/><circle cx="330" cy="28" r="4.5"/>
        <circle cx="600" cy="212" r="4.5"/><circle cx="600" cy="166" r="4.5"/><circle cx="560" cy="120" r="4.5"/><circle cx="640" cy="120" r="4.5"/><circle cx="600" cy="74" r="4.5"/><circle cx="600" cy="28" r="4.5"/>
      </g>
      <g class="lbl" fill="currentColor" font-size="12.5">
        <!-- B_3 element labels -->
        <text x="110" y="214" text-anchor="middle">∅</text>
        <text x="36" y="144" text-anchor="end">1</text><text x="119" y="153" text-anchor="start">2</text><text x="184" y="144" text-anchor="start">3</text>
        <text x="36" y="88" text-anchor="end">12</text><text x="119" y="80" text-anchor="start">13</text><text x="184" y="88" text-anchor="start">23</text>
        <text x="110" y="18" text-anchor="middle">123</text>
        <!-- B_3/S_3: orbit sizes -->
        <text x="344" y="200" text-anchor="start">∅</text>
        <text x="344" y="144" text-anchor="start">&#123;1&#125;</text>
        <text x="344" y="88" text-anchor="start">&#123;1,2&#125;</text>
        <text x="344" y="32" text-anchor="start">&#123;1,2,3&#125;</text>
        <!-- B_4/C_4 labels -->
        <text x="614" y="216" text-anchor="start">∅</text>
        <text x="614" y="170" text-anchor="start">&#123;1&#125;</text>
        <text x="546" y="124" text-anchor="end">&#123;1,2&#125;</text>
        <text x="654" y="124" text-anchor="start">&#123;1,3&#125;</text>
        <text x="614" y="78" text-anchor="start">&#123;1,2,3&#125;</text>
        <text x="614" y="32" text-anchor="start">&#123;1,2,3,4&#125;</text>
      </g>
      <g class="mult" font-size="11">
        <!-- cover multiplicities of U on orbit sums -->
        <text x="322" y="171" text-anchor="end">1</text>
        <text x="322" y="115" text-anchor="end">2</text>
        <text x="322" y="59" text-anchor="end">3</text>
        <text x="592" y="192" text-anchor="end">1</text>
        <text x="574" y="148" text-anchor="end">2</text>
        <text x="626" y="148" text-anchor="start">2</text>
        <text x="574" y="94" text-anchor="end">2</text>
        <text x="626" y="94" text-anchor="start">1</text>
        <text x="592" y="54" text-anchor="end">4</text>
      </g>
      <g class="cap" font-size="11.5">
        <text x="110" y="232" text-anchor="middle">B₃</text>
        <text x="330" y="232" text-anchor="middle">B₃ / S₃</text>
        <text x="600" y="232" text-anchor="middle">B₄ / C₄ — necklaces, 4 beads, 2 colors</text>
      </g>
    </svg>
    <figcaption>
      B<sub>3</sub>, its quotient by <i>S</i><sub>3</sub>, and the quotient of{' '}
      B<sub>4</sub> by the 4-cycle. In a quotient, each cover edge carries the
      coefficient with which Stanley’s up-operator <i>U</i> sends one orbit sum
      to the next: <i>U</i>(1 + 2 + 3) = 2·(12 + 13 + 23), so the middle edge
      of the chain is labelled 2. The maps below draw exactly these numbers.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="prose-col">
    <p>
      The reason to care is Stanley’s theorem that any quotient{' '}
      B<sub><i>n</i></sub>/<i>G</i> is rank-symmetric, rank-unimodal, and
      Sperner: the largest antichain is a rank, whatever the group. The proof
      lifts the linear-algebra method — order-matchings extracted from the
      up-operator — to the invariants, and the multiplicities on the edges
      are precisely the matrix of <i>U</i> restricted to those invariants.
      The finite-space map is the instructive exception: the poset of
      topologies is not graded, its covers skip rows, and the theorem has
      nothing to say about it.
      Each map lays the quotient out so that the order-reversing symmetry of
      the original (complementing a graph, swapping the colors of a necklace,
      Alexander duality for a complex) is a reflection of the picture, colors
      the elements by orbit size, and opens a panel with the stabilizer, the
      covers, and the dual for whatever you click.
    </p>
  </div>

  <ul class="gallery" style="margin-top:var(--s7)">
    {maps.map((m) => (
      <li class="card">
        <a class="frame" href={m.href} aria-label={m.title}>
          <img src={m.thumb} alt="" loading="lazy" width="800" height="600" />
        </a>
        <h3><a href={m.href}>{m.title}</a></h3>
        <p class="formula label" set:html={m.formula} />
        <p set:html={m.blurb} />
        <div class="tags label">
          {m.tags.map((t) => <span>{t}</span>)}
        </div>
      </li>
    ))}
  </ul>

  <p class="dim see-also">
    See also <a href="/sperner-chains/">Sperner chains</a>, which partitions{' '}
    B<sub><i>n</i></sub> itself into chains by the same linear-algebra method,
    and the <a href="/372fall26/">Math 372</a> schedule, where these were built.
  </p>
</Base>

<style>
  .prose-col { max-width: var(--measure); }
  .prose-col p + p { margin-top: var(--s4); }

  .hasse-fig {
    margin: var(--s7) 0;
    max-width: 50rem;
  }
  .hasse-fig svg {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    color: var(--ink);
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    overflow: visible;
  }
  .hasse-fig .lbl text { fill: var(--ink); }
  .hasse-fig .mult text { fill: var(--accent); font-weight: 500; }
  .hasse-fig .cap text { fill: var(--muted); letter-spacing: 0.04em; }
  .hasse-fig .arrow { color: var(--muted); }
  .hasse-fig figcaption {
    margin-top: var(--s4);
    max-width: var(--measure);
    font-size: 0.92rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .card .formula { text-transform: none; letter-spacing: 0.02em; font-size: 0.78rem; }
  .see-also { margin-top: var(--s7); max-width: var(--measure); }
</style>
